
.TH HOSTID 1 "October 2026" "hostid" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
hostid \- print the numeric identifier of the current host
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hostid
.RB [ \-h ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B hostid
prints the numeric identifier of the current host
as an eight-digit hexadecimal number.
.PP
The identifier is obtained using the
.BR gethostid (3)
function and represents a 32-bit value associated
with the system.
.PP
On Linux systems using glibc, the identifier is normally
read from
.B /etc/hostid
if that file exists.
Otherwise, the library attempts to derive an identifier
from the host's network address.
.PP
Host identifiers were historically used for software
licensing and machine identification.
They may also be used by software such as OpenZFS
to identify the system associated with a storage pool.
.PP
The host identifier is not guaranteed to be unique
and should not be used as a secure machine identity.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Display usage information and exit.
.SH EXAMPLES
Display the host identifier:
.PP
.RS
.B hostid
.RE
.PP
Example output:
.PP
.RS
.B 007f0101
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Successful completion.
.TP
.B 1
An invalid option or argument was specified,
or an output error occurred.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /etc/hostid
Optional file containing the system's host identifier,
used by glibc when available.
.SH NOTES
The host identifier is distinct from the hostname
and from the machine identifier stored in
.BR /etc/machine-id .
.PP
The
.B hostid
utility does not modify the host identifier.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR hostname (1),
.BR gethostid (3),
.BR sethostid (3),
.BR machine-id (5)

